Crawl's Hell is an optional area.

(There are lots of optional areas in Crawl, and it's one of the ways in
which Crawlers "dial-a-difficulty", by the way, which could be a
solution to a too-easy Nethack, although as a casual observer of
Nethackdom, I'm surprised that its highly developed Conducts system
isn't considered sufficiently potent as a difficulty-dialer.)

The Four Hells are quite similar in some important respects (they all
have randomly timed "torments", which Do Bad Things to you and tend to
summon auto-awake nasties. All up staircases in all of them take you
all the way back to the foyer, forcing you to redive the whole branch
(albeit now with the map information and monster-clearing you've
achieved). With certain exceptions, they never have items except in the
treasure hoards in the fixed vaults at their ends that contain their
bosses. And they are as a group generally considered about the third
hardest area in the game.

They then fall into a loose grouping of "the anti-potion hells" and the
"anti-scroll hells", i.e. you'll tend to lose scrolls right and left in
two, and potions right and left in the other two. Certain scrolls and
potions are painful to do without (e.g. Crawl's closest (still-vague)
equivalent to an AoLS comes in scroll form), so this gives each group a
certain flavor specific to that group.

Each of the Hells specifically has its own flavor and some are more
difficult than others overall (with rewards to match), as modified by
how well your character jibes with them (e.g. an ice magician, barring
a strong investment in non-ice magic, can forget about the icy Hell).
They do suffer a wee bit of sameness within their groups and within the
frame of Hell overall due to the factors mentioned above, though.

